# D-mealz backend (Supabase)

The backend is a managed Supabase project: Postgres + Realtime. There is no
server code to deploy.

```
backend/
  supabase/schema.sql   <- run once in the Supabase SQL Editor
```

## What schema.sql creates
| Object | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `public.records (coll, id, data jsonb, updated_at)` | One table holding every shared record. `coll` is the collection, `id` the record key, `data` the JSON. |
| `records_coll_ok` check | Only the app's 15 collections are accepted: sent, fb, msgs, notes, dmsg, conf, chat, reqs, att, ov, pick, prof, acc, votes, vw |
| `records_size_ok` check | Rejects any record over 200 KB |
| `touch_updated_at` trigger | Keeps `updated_at` current |
| RLS policies | Allow the browser (anon key) to select, insert, update, delete |
| Realtime publication | Pushes changes to every open device |

## Setup
1. https://supabase.com -> New project.
2. SQL Editor -> New query -> paste `supabase/schema.sql` -> Run.
3. Project Settings -> API: copy **Project URL** and **anon public key**.
4. Put them in `frontend/js/config.js`.

## Collections (what each stores)
| coll | Contents |
|---|---|
| votes | Weekly menu votes, one per student per meal slot |
| sent | Surplus-food alerts and partner accept/decline replies |
| fb | Student feedback and ratings |
| msgs, dmsg | Mess / institution / donor messages |
| notes | Menu-change notifications |
| conf | Pickup confirmations |
| chat | Role-to-role chat |
| reqs, pick | Monthly special-menu requests and the chosen item |
| att | Attendance counts |
| ov | Dining-admin menu overrides |
| prof | User profiles |
| acc | Account records (salt + password hash) |
| vw | Voting window (open/close time) |

## Security
The app signs people in itself and talks to Supabase with the public anon
key, so the RLS policies are open by design. Anyone holding the site and key
can read or write every record, including `acc`. Use for demos and pilots
only. To harden: adopt Supabase Auth, add a `user_id` column, and rewrite
the policies with `auth.uid()`; keep `acc` and `prof` owner-only.